WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.580
Defensive coding patterns make lifetime behavior easier to inspect.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:10.520
They do not replace design, but they reduce accidental reuse and make cleanup decisions visible.

3
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:18.940
The best patterns are boring in a good way: predictable initialization, clear ownership flags, consistent cleanup, and limited control-flow surprises.

4
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:23.220
Initialize pointers before use and keep cleanup state explicit.

5
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:29.560
A pointer that starts as null is easier for cleanup code to handle than an uninitialized value.

6
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:36.460
An initialized flag, ownership flag, or structured resource state can help cleanup routines know what has actually been acquired.

7
00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:41.220
Centralize release logic when repeated cleanup would create confusion.

8
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:46.920
A shared cleanup path can release resources in a known order and reduce duplicated code.

9
00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.500
It should still be easy to tell which resources are valid at each point.

10
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.120
Centralization helps most when state is explicit.

11
00:00:55.400 --> 00:01:01.240
Setting a pointer to null after freeing can help prevent accidental reuse through that specific variable.

12
00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:06.700
It is a useful local habit, especially for objects that may continue through cleanup code.

13
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.080
But it does not solve aliasing across the whole program.

14
00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:13.380
Other aliases may still contain the old address.

15
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:18.860
Make invalid states harder to represent with clearer structures and ownership flags where appropriate.

16
00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.720
A buffer object can pair pointer, capacity, count, and ownership.

17
00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.540
A handle type can require cleanup through one routine.

18
00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.200
A container can state whether it owns elements.

19
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.540
These patterns reduce ambiguity.

20
00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:37.380
Prefer simple, reviewable lifetime logic over clever control flow.

21
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:45.560
Complicated jumps, hidden ownership side effects, and surprising release behavior increase the chance that a later maintainer will use a pointer after release.

22
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.660
Clear code is not only easier to read; it is easier to trust.
